<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zwaanendael</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1631">1631</a>–<a href="/years/1632">1632</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Zwaanendael Colony</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zwaanendael was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> whaling and farming settlement founded in 1631 near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lewes-delaware">Lewes, Delaware</a>, by patroons of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-west-india-company">Dutch West India Company</a>. Within about a year a quarrel with the local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lenape">Lenape</a> led to its destruction and the killing of all its settlers. Though short-lived, it gave the Dutch a claim to the Delaware valley, and Delaware dates its first European settlement to it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zwaanendael.jpg" alt="Zwaanendael" width="320" height="225" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Johannes Vingboons · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Delaware_Bay_Vinckeboons_14.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The settlement stood on Blommaert's Kill, now Lewes Creek, a little north of Cape Henlopen, on the bay the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> named Godyn's Bay, now Delaware Bay. Its name is archaic Dutch for swan valley. In 1629 Samuel Blommaert and Samuel Godyn, directors of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a> chamber of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-west-india-company">Dutch West India Company</a>, bargained with the native inhabitants for a tract reaching from Cape Henlopen to the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delaware-river">Delaware River</a>, the oldest deed for land in Delaware. Peter Minuit and his council at Fort Amsterdam ratified the purchase in 1630.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The partners in the venture included Blommaert, Godyn, Kiliaen van Rensselaer, the geographer Joannes de Laet, David Pietersen de Vries and Mathijs Jansen Van Keulen. They fitted out the eighteen-gun ship Walvis to bring the colonists and defend the coast, with whaling to help meet the costs. A colony of twenty-eight people under Gillis Hosset built a palisaded fort with the red lion of Holland fixed to its gate.</p><p>According to an account later given to de Vries by Nanticoke Indians, the trouble began when a chief took a piece of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a> painted with the arms of Holland to make tobacco pipes. The settlers protested so strongly that his own people killed him, and his friends then took revenge: three men came to barter bear skins, killed the man in charge, a sick man and the chained mastiff, and then struck down the others at their work.</p><p>De Vries reached the charred remains on 5 December 1632 with about 50 more colonists. He negotiated a treaty with the Indians and found provisions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a>, but the massacre led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> to retrench, and he soon moved the colonists to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-amsterdam">New Amsterdam</a>. The Zwaanendael claims were resold to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-west-india-company">Dutch West India Company</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The site of the settlement later became the town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lewes-delaware">Lewes, Delaware</a>, and the colony is held to have laid the foundation of the state of Delaware and defined its small area, most of which lay within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> purchase. A monument to the colony, the De Vries Palisade, was dedicated there on 22 September 1909, and the Zwaanendael Museum opened in 1931.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Zwaanendael Colony” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On the Delaware the patroons' settlement of <b>Zwaanendael</b> was destroyed by the local Indigenous population soon after its founding in 1631, and in 1638 Peter Minuit, now in Swedish service, planted New Sweden at Fort Christina; New Amsterdam could only protest.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-netherland">New Netherland · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Dutch settlement of <b>Zwaanendael</b> near modern Lewes was destroyed in 1631, and in 1638 New Sweden built Fort Christina at what is now Wilmington.</blockquote><a href="/state/delaware-colony">Delaware Colony</a></li></ul></section>
